---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const shares = assertion('seventy-per-cent-farmed-and-nineteen-developed')
const stable = assertion('ninety-seven-and-a-half-per-cent-did-not-change')
const control = assertion('the-boundary-is-the-control')

const pasture = assertion('pasture-is-the-class-that-fell')
const forest = assertion('eight-acres-of-forest-in-twenty-years')
const built = assertion('nineteen-hundred-acres-in-and-sixteen-hundred-off-farmland')
const thicken = assertion('the-county-thickened-rather-than-spread')
const largest = assertion('the-largest-transition-is-not-farm-to-town')
const denser = assertion('more-acres-got-denser-than-got-built')
const paved = assertion('six-per-cent-of-the-county-is-paved')
const hard = assertion('the-thickening-paved-as-much-as-the-building')

const oneway = assertion('not-one-acre-moved-the-other-way')
const pixel = assertion('a-pixel-is-not-a-building')

const swampLeft = assertion('four-per-cent-of-the-swamp-is-still-wet')
const drainage = assertion('the-drainage-was-ninety-six-per-cent-complete')
const marsh = assertion('two-instruments-disagree-about-marsh-by-eighteen')

const producer = assertion('the-producer-named-the-file-to-use-instead')
const recode = assertion('four-columns-that-were-never-a-second-opinion')
const boundary = assertion('one-epoch-boundary-is-a-mapping-change')
const category = assertion('a-category-is-not-a-land-use')

const classes: [string, number][] = [
  ['Cultivated crops', 176619],
  ['Developed', 48694],
  ['Forest', 21226],
  ['Pasture and hay', 7315],
  ['Open water', 2607],
  ['Grassland', 1934],
  ['Wetland', 1731],
  ['Barren and shrub', 256],
]

const devChange: [string, number][] = [
  ['Open space', -925],
  ['Low intensity', 343],
  ['Medium intensity', 1748],
  ['High intensity', 742],
]

const moves: [string, number][] = [
  ['Developed open space → medium', 1020],
  ['Pasture → cultivated crops', 802],
  ['Crops → developed open space', 480],
  ['Crops → developed medium', 428],
  ['Developed open space → low', 395],
  ['Crops → open water', 393],
  ['Crops → developed low', 333],
  ['Crops → pasture', 265],
  ['Developed low → medium', 262],
  ['Crops → developed high', 248],
  ['Developed low → high', 227],
  ['Pasture → deciduous forest', 210],
]

const classesSpec = bar(
  classes.map(([n]) => n),
  classes.map(([, a]) => a),
  { yTitle: 'Acres, 2021', labelValues: true },
)
const devSpec = bar(
  devChange.map(([n]) => n),
  devChange.map(([, a]) => a),
  { yTitle: 'Change in acres, 2001 to 2021', labelValues: true },
)
const movesSpec = bar(
  moves.map(([n]) => n),
  moves.map(([, a]) => a),
  { yTitle: 'Acres changing class, 2001 to 2021', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="the-ground-that-did-not-move">
  <p>
    What Allen County's ground is has been described here from one file and one year — a crop
    classification that also carries columns for developed land, forest, water and wetland, under a
    standing rule saying those four columns may not be differenced from one year to the next. The
    rule was arrived at by a control: the developed column falls thirty-five acres a year in a
    county that has been building the whole time.
  </p>
  <p>
    The producer of that file says the same thing on its own page, and names the remedy. Its
    non-agricultural classes, it explains, are entirely dependent on the National Land Cover
    Database, and users doing that sort of work should consider the database instead. So here is the
    database: nine classifications of every thirty-metre pixel in the county, 2001 to 2021, clipped
    and counted.
  </p>

  <h2>What the ground is</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={shares} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="classes"
    title="Allen County by land cover class, 2021"
    caption="Cultivated crops are two thirds of the county on their own. The last four classes together are under two and a half per cent."
    columns={['Class', 'Acres']}
    rows={classes.map(([n, a]) => [n, a.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="classes" set:html={JSON.stringify(classesSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={stable} />
  <p>
    That is the finding a nine-epoch series exists to produce and a single year cannot. Two decades
    of a county whose story is loss, and ninety-seven and a half per cent of its surface is filed
    under the same word at the end as at the beginning.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={control} />

  <h2>What did move</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={pasture} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={forest} />
  <p>
    Eight acres in twenty years is not a photograph of a still county. Forest gained four hundred
    and thirty-four acres and lost four hundred and forty-two across the eight steps between — trees
    coming in on ground that stopped being grazed, going out under houses, and very nearly cancelling.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={built} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={thicken} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="developed"
    title="Change in Allen County's developed classes, 2001 to 2021"
    caption="The lightest class shrank while the three denser ones grew. Total developed land rose 1,909 acres."
    columns={['Developed class', 'Change in acres']}
    rows={devChange.map(([n, a]) => [n, a > 0 ? `+${a.toLocaleString()}` : a.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="developed" set:html={JSON.stringify(devSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={largest} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="transitions"
    title="The largest land cover transitions, 2001 to 2021"
    caption="Six of the twelve largest moves are within the developed classes or into them. The crops-to-open-water row is a mapping change, and is discussed below."
    columns={['From → to', 'Acres']}
    rows={moves.map(([n, a]) => [n, a.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="transitions" set:html={JSON.stringify(movesSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={denser} />
  <p>
    A county that has been losing people since 1980 did not sprawl. It filled in what it had: lawns
    and verges and thin edges became parking, roofs and road, and the actual frontier of building
    moved less than the interior did.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={paved} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={hard} />

  <h2>A class that only moves one way</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={oneway} open />
  <p>
    Two readings fit that and the file settles neither. The county built and never un-built, which
    is plausible in direction and hard to believe in degree — a demolished block, a closed plant on
    a reverting lot, a quarry gone to grass ought to leave some pixels going the other way. Or the
    product enforces it, on the reasonable ground that reversals are usually classification noise.
    Arguing from plausibility cannot separate them, because the plausible argument is exactly the one
    a product designer would have made. So the 1,909 acres are published as a ceiling on the net
    change and not as a gross figure.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={pixel} />

  <h2>What the swamp came to</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={swampLeft} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={drainage} />
  <p>
    The largest single fact about this county's ground is that it was drained. Robert Gordon's map
    of the original vegetation puts 22,433.9 acres of elm-ash swamp forest inside the county line;
    what a satellite finds on the same ground now is 973 acres of woody wetland. The two are not the
    same measurement and the order of magnitude between them is the answer.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={marsh} />

  <h2>The file the manual named</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={producer} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={recode} />
  <p>
    Reading the two files against each other is not two instruments agreeing or disagreeing about
    Allen County. It is one instrument and a copy of it on a different cycle — which is why the
    5,811-acre gap on forest is a fact about the recode and not about any trees.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={boundary} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={category} />
</Article>
